        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterson%2C_Utah",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first public building, a combined school and LDS church, opened in Peterson in 1861.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterson%2C_Utah",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Peterson served as Morgan County’s seat from 1862 until 1866, then briefly for Littleton before Morgan took over in 1868.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterson%2C_Utah",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1872 the town’s name changed to Peterson to honor early settler Charles Sreeve Peterson.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterson%2C_Utah",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Union Pacific Railroad built Peterson Station, turning the community into a key rail stop in the region.",
